WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.799
Most agencies run ScubaGear,
save the HTML report,

2
00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:06.450
and reopen it when an assessor asks.

3
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.843
The program treats each tool as an adapter
instead: one input to one evidence chain,

4
00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:15.650
with the tools left exactly as
their publishers ship them.

5
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.063
Each run flows through four stages.

6
00:00:18.063 --> 00:00:20.000
The tool writes its raw output.

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.537
Each result becomes a claim, stamped with
who issued it, when, and a lineage hash.

8
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:28.663
Claims from one interval
are sealed into a bundle.

9
00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:33.051
And the bundle emits OSCAL
assessment results and POA&M items.

10
00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:37.738
Take one example: the policy that
blocks legacy authentication fails.

11
00:00:37.738 --> 00:00:42.175
The claim ties back to the raw result,
and the bundle opens a POA&M item.

12
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:45.345
Months later, an assessor
can rebuild the bundle and

13
00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:48.737
walk back to the raw output
without re-running anything.

14
00:00:48.737 --> 00:00:51.899
The limits are stated too:
it runs on an interval,

15
00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:54.250
and Google Workspace is still a gap.

16
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.100
Next, turning findings into a program.
